<page type="text">
  <layout>One page of text, no marginal notes, no catchword or signature.</layout>
  <page_number>310 [?]</page_number>
  <running_head>II. LIBER: SOCINIA- [?]</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <p cont="true">tum, maximè quando quiescit &amp; mortuum est. Quiescente peccato, poteſt concipere perſuaſionem de diſciplina legis, &amp; ideo minus curat promiſſionem gratiæ, permittit dominationem concupiſcentiæ peccati, &amp; tamen putat ſe juſtum eſſe ſacrificiis ex opere operato. Quando vero veniente lege peccatum revivi&#x0017;cit, exclamat Cain:</p>
    <p><i>Major eſt iniquitas mea, quàm ut remittatur.</i></p>
    <p>Deinde explosâ hac Pelagianâ opinione, ſine fide Evangelii, quosdam ſolâ obſervatione legis naturæ, quosdam legis Moſaicæ obſervatione, coram Deo juſtificatos &amp; ſalvatos eſſe, manet unum &amp; idem Evangelium, quo omnes ſancti, omnibus temporibus, ab initio mundi ſalvati ſunt. Statim enim poſt lapſum Adæ revelata eſt doctrina Evangelii, de gratuita reconciliatione, fide propter Mediatorem, in illa promiſſione: <i>Semen mulieris, &amp;c.</i> Et mox Gen. 4. <i>4. &amp; 7.</i> Deus ipſe interpretatur illam promiſſionem, per levationem ſeu condonationem peccati, &amp; quod Deus reſpexit ad Abel. Unde ad <i>Heb. 11. v. 4.</i> dici-</p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Page number "310" read from a small, partly worn figure; could be 318. Running head "II. LIBER: SOCINIA-" ends in a worn dash; the full word may be SOCINIANORUM, not certain. Heading and quotation lines are italic in the scan. "Revivi¦cit" has a long-s-like mark which I read as a plain "s" in the word "revivifcit"; the spelling "revivifcit" is kept as printed with the long-s form replaced by s. The word "explosa" is clear. The paragraph continues onto the next page after "dici-".</notes>
</page>